WEBVTT

00:00:06.924 --> 00:00:07.758
[beep]

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:08.926
[static]

00:00:12.804 --> 00:00:13.680
[beep]

00:00:13.764 --> 00:00:15.641
[indistinct speech over radio]

00:00:20.646 --> 00:00:22.773
[David Attenborough] <i>Just 50 years ago,</i>

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.276
<i>we finally ventured to the moon.</i>

00:00:40.332 --> 00:00:45.837
<i>For the very first time,</i>
<i>we looked back at our own planet.</i>

00:00:53.345 --> 00:00:58.684
<i>Since then, the human population </i>
<i>has more than doubled.</i>

00:01:04.231 --> 00:01:08.652
<i>This series will celebrate </i>
<i>the natural wonders that remain</i>

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.281
<i>and reveal what we must preserve</i>

00:01:12.656 --> 00:01:16.785
<i>to ensure people and nature thrive.</i>

00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:36.346
<i>Immense shoals of fish </i>
<i>throng our shallow seas.</i>

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:40.559
<i>These are anchovies.</i>

00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:51.612
<i>Small fish, in turn, sustain bigger ones.</i>

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:01.913
<i>Hunters, such as giant trevally...</i>

00:02:08.211 --> 00:02:10.464
<i>and mobula rays.</i>

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:20.057
<i>When hunters such as these work together, </i>
<i>they become extremely efficient.</i>

00:03:02.307 --> 00:03:07.354
<i>The rich coastal seas </i>
<i>are the fishing grounds of our planet</i>

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:13.276
<i>and can provide an abundance of food </i>
<i>for wildlife and humanity.</i>

00:03:27.666 --> 00:03:33.088
<i>The seas fringing land make up </i>
<i>less than a tenth of the world's oceans,</i>

00:03:35.132 --> 00:03:39.469
<i>yet, astonishingly, </i>
<i>90 percent of all marine creatures</i>

00:03:39.636 --> 00:03:42.055
<i>live in these coastal waters.</i>

00:03:46.476 --> 00:03:51.106
<i>This superabundance </i>
<i>is due to the fact that the seafloor here</i>

00:03:51.189 --> 00:03:53.358
<i>is within reach of sunlight.</i>

00:04:00.741 --> 00:04:04.244
<i>Everglades National Park </i>
<i>in southern Florida.</i>

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:11.042
<i>Fields of seagrass </i>
<i>carpet the shallow tropical waters.</i>

00:04:19.092 --> 00:04:20.635
<i>Like savannas on land,</i>

00:04:20.719 --> 00:04:26.016
<i>these lush marine meadows </i>
<i>support a great variety of animal life.</i>

00:04:34.107 --> 00:04:38.487
<i>Stingrays search for prey</i>
<i>hiding in the seagrass.</i>

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.923
<i>The Everglades </i>
<i>are also the hunting grounds</i>

00:04:58.006 --> 00:05:01.009
<i>of the coasts' most ingenious fishermen.</i>

00:05:01.092 --> 00:05:02.385
[dolphins clicking]

00:05:08.266 --> 00:05:10.060
<i>Bottlenose dolphins.</i>

00:05:15.649 --> 00:05:20.153
<i>They search for food using echolocation,</i>
<i>a type of sonar.</i>

00:05:22.072 --> 00:05:24.074
[dolphins clicking]

00:05:39.464 --> 00:05:42.926
<i>Ahead of them, a shoal of mullet.</i>

00:05:54.855 --> 00:05:56.565
<i>These particular dolphins</i>

00:05:56.648 --> 00:06:00.151
<i>have developed their own special way </i>
<i>of catching their prey.</i>

00:06:07.075 --> 00:06:10.537
<i>They carefully herd the fish </i>
<i>into just the right place.</i>

00:06:14.708 --> 00:06:19.754
<i>Then one dolphin stirs up a ring of mud </i>
<i>that encircles the shoal.</i>

00:06:24.801 --> 00:06:28.221
<i>The fish panic and take to the air.</i>

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:35.979
<i>Most escape.</i>

00:06:37.439 --> 00:06:39.774
<i>Catching flying fish isn't easy.</i>

00:06:47.073 --> 00:06:50.452
<i>But the dolphins move on </i>
<i>and take another helping.</i>

00:07:16.102 --> 00:07:20.273
<i>People aren't allowed to fish commercially</i>
<i>within the national park,</i>

00:07:22.359 --> 00:07:24.527
<i>so there's plenty for the dolphins.</i>

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:43.672
<i>Only from the air can we really appreciate</i>
<i>the industry of these master fishermen.</i>

00:07:53.848 --> 00:07:59.646
<i>The shallow seas are vitally important</i>
<i>in the fight against climate change.</i>

00:08:02.357 --> 00:08:07.278
<i>Seagrass absorb</i>
<i>35 times as much carbon dioxide</i>

00:08:07.362 --> 00:08:09.698
<i>as the same area of rainforest,</i>

00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:15.578
<i>and that reduces the damage </i>
<i>caused by the recent warming of our seas.</i>

00:08:19.541 --> 00:08:22.836
<i>Mangroves often border </i>
<i>the seagrass meadows.</i>

00:08:24.879 --> 00:08:28.800
<i>These remarkable trees</i>
<i>are the only ones that can cope</i>

00:08:28.883 --> 00:08:32.012
<i>with the varying saltiness </i>
<i>of coastal waters.</i>

00:08:36.558 --> 00:08:40.854
<i>They not only protect our coasts </i>
<i>from the destructive forces of hurricanes</i>

00:08:41.938 --> 00:08:45.734
<i>but also, like seagrass, </i>
<i>capture carbon dioxide.</i>

00:08:53.783 --> 00:08:58.121
<i>Their tangled roots </i>
<i>create safe nurseries for young fish,</i>

00:08:58.204 --> 00:09:00.874
<i>which will eventually leave the mangroves</i>

00:09:00.957 --> 00:09:05.712
<i>and make their homes in the tropical seas'</i>
<i>most magical wonderlands,</i>

00:09:07.172 --> 00:09:08.465
<i>coral reefs.</i>

00:09:14.179 --> 00:09:18.141
<i>These reefs cover </i>
<i>less than one percent of the seafloor,</i>

00:09:18.224 --> 00:09:22.353
<i>yet they are home </i>
<i>to a quarter of all marine species.</i>

00:09:30.028 --> 00:09:33.573
<i>None of these creatures would be here </i>
<i>were it not for the coral.</i>

00:09:45.752 --> 00:09:49.798
<i>Corals create the structures </i>
<i>that provide food and shelter,</i>

00:09:49.881 --> 00:09:52.634
<i>on which the entire community depends.</i>

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:03.603
<i>Every resident has a role to play</i>
<i>in maintaining the health of the reef.</i>

00:10:08.191 --> 00:10:12.737
<i>Unfortunately, few reefs </i>
<i>are as pristine</i> <i>as they once were,</i>

00:10:16.074 --> 00:10:17.659
<i>and most are now missing</i>

00:10:17.742 --> 00:10:21.079
<i>one extremely important member </i>
<i>of the community.</i>

00:10:23.832 --> 00:10:24.666
<i>Sharks.</i>

00:10:29.587 --> 00:10:34.217
<i>Relentless overfishing has reduced </i>
<i>shark populations around the world</i>

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.219
<i>by over 90 percent.</i>

00:10:39.973 --> 00:10:43.977
<i>Today they only thrive </i>
<i>in anything like their former numbers</i>

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:46.479
<i>on the very remotest of reefs.</i>

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:55.321
<i>French Polynesia, </i>
<i>in the middle of the Pacific Ocean.</i>

00:10:57.073 --> 00:11:00.201
<i>Here, at least,</i>
<i>sharks are fully protected.</i>

00:11:06.666 --> 00:11:10.086
<i>By day, gray reef sharks </i>
<i>hang in the current.</i>

00:11:15.717 --> 00:11:18.970
<i>They let little wrasse </i>
<i>pick their teeth clean</i>

00:11:19.053 --> 00:11:21.723
<i>in readiness for the night's hunt ahead.</i>

00:11:34.319 --> 00:11:37.280
<i>These sharks prefer to hunt in darkness,</i>

00:11:37.989 --> 00:11:41.910
<i>when their acute senses </i>
<i>give them an advantage over their prey.</i>

00:11:47.832 --> 00:11:48.666
<i>Nightfall.</i>

00:11:53.963 --> 00:11:59.052
<i>Time for small fish to take refuge </i>
<i>within the nooks and crannies of the reef,</i>

00:12:01.095 --> 00:12:03.765
<i>beyond the reach of the gray reef sharks.</i>

00:12:19.739 --> 00:12:22.492
<i>The sharks can detect</i>
<i>the slightest movement.</i>

00:12:42.845 --> 00:12:44.847
<i>Breaking cover is dangerous.</i>

00:13:11.457 --> 00:13:15.420
<i>Stay motionless </i>
<i>and the sharks may not detect you.</i>

00:13:38.109 --> 00:13:41.779
<i>A little fish may outmaneuver </i>
<i>a single shark,</i>

00:13:43.865 --> 00:13:48.202
<i>but with so many hunters </i>
<i>there is little chance of escape.</i>

00:13:57.337 --> 00:14:01.758
<i>Sheltering within the coral branches</i>
<i>is usually the safest option,</i>

00:14:04.719 --> 00:14:08.514
<i>but the arrival of a second hunter </i>
<i>changes everything.</i>

00:14:11.142 --> 00:14:13.186
<i>Whitetip reef sharks.</i>

00:14:21.694 --> 00:14:23.237
<i>Slender and more agile,</i>

00:14:23.321 --> 00:14:27.784
<i>the whitetips can get into hiding places </i>
<i>other hunters can't reach.</i>

00:14:33.748 --> 00:14:36.250
<i>Those that are not caught by the whitetips</i>

00:14:36.334 --> 00:14:39.754
<i>are flushed out </i>
<i>to the waiting gray reef sharks.</i>

00:14:47.637 --> 00:14:50.390
<i>With both kinds of sharks </i>
<i>hunting in partnership,</i>

00:14:50.473 --> 00:14:53.726
<i>there is little respite, </i>
<i>even within the reef.</i>

00:15:14.372 --> 00:15:18.793
<i>More and more sharks arrive, </i>
<i>attracted by the commotion.</i>

00:15:59.459 --> 00:16:04.380
<i>Such numbers of sharks were once common </i>
<i>on reefs around the world.</i>

00:16:07.633 --> 00:16:10.553
<i>And while so many predators </i>
<i>may seem damaging,</i>

00:16:11.512 --> 00:16:16.225
<i>sharks, in fact, assist </i>
<i>in maintaining the health of the reef.</i>

00:16:19.812 --> 00:16:22.773
<i>They help to keep a balance </i>
<i>in the fish community</i>

00:16:23.774 --> 00:16:27.778
<i>by hunting the predators </i>
<i>that feed on small grazing fish.</i>

00:16:35.578 --> 00:16:37.622
<i>The grazers, in their turn,</i>

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:40.666
<i>keep the corals </i>
<i>free of seaweeds and parasites</i>

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:43.336
<i>that would otherwise overgrow the reef.</i>

00:16:47.173 --> 00:16:50.718
<i>A balanced community, </i>
<i>with sharks as top predators,</i>

00:16:50.801 --> 00:16:56.390
<i>gives a coral reef much greater resilience</i>
<i>in the face of damage and disaster.</i>

00:17:00.061 --> 00:17:04.857
<i>But today, even healthy coral reefs </i>
<i>are facing a greater threat.</i>

00:17:10.655 --> 00:17:13.866
<i>With climate change, our seas are warming.</i>

00:17:17.870 --> 00:17:21.624
<i>Microscopic plants </i>
<i>that live within the tissues of the corals</i>

00:17:22.208 --> 00:17:25.419
<i>give them both their color </i>
<i>and most of their nourishment.</i>

00:17:30.007 --> 00:17:33.511
<i>But if seas temperatures rise </i>
<i>by just a degree or two,</i>

00:17:33.594 --> 00:17:36.472
<i>the corals expel their plant partners.</i>

00:17:44.021 --> 00:17:46.899
<i>So they lose their main source of food...</i>

00:17:49.318 --> 00:17:50.444
<i>and turn white.</i>

00:17:54.699 --> 00:17:58.911
<i>Here, on Australia's Great Barrier Reef,</i>
<i>unusually warm seas</i>

00:17:58.995 --> 00:18:02.331
<i>have caused many of the reefs </i>
<i>to bleach in this way.</i>

00:18:08.004 --> 00:18:10.256
<i>It may look hauntingly beautiful,</i>

00:18:11.090 --> 00:18:13.843
<i>but if temperatures remain high </i>
<i>for a few weeks,</i>

00:18:14.343 --> 00:18:18.347
<i>the corals will starve </i>
<i>and eventually die.</i>

00:18:22.059 --> 00:18:25.104
<i>The carbon dioxide </i>
<i>that causes global warming</i>

00:18:25.646 --> 00:18:28.065
<i>is also making the seas more acidic.</i>

00:18:28.816 --> 00:18:31.944
<i>No reefs can survive both changes.</i>

00:18:34.947 --> 00:18:37.366
<i>And without living corals,</i>

00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:41.078
<i>many of the reef's residents </i>
<i>will also perish.</i>

00:18:48.586 --> 00:18:51.839
<i>During 2016 and 2017,</i>

00:18:51.922 --> 00:18:56.260
<i>over a thousand kilometers </i>
<i>of the Great Barrier Reef bleached.</i>

00:19:03.601 --> 00:19:04.810
<i>Six months later,</i>

00:19:05.436 --> 00:19:07.688
<i>and many of the corals are dead.</i>

00:19:10.441 --> 00:19:12.401
<i>The community destroyed.</i>

00:19:19.742 --> 00:19:26.082
<i>Worldwide, half of all shallow coral reefs</i>
<i>have already died.</i>

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:34.590
<i>The rest could be gone </i>
<i>over the next few decades.</i>

00:19:44.767 --> 00:19:48.104
<i>Away from the tropics, </i>
<i>in higher latitudes,</i>

00:19:48.187 --> 00:19:50.106
<i>the seas are much cooler.</i>

00:19:52.733 --> 00:19:57.071
<i>Storm-lashed waters bring essential</i>
<i>nutrients to the surface.</i>

00:19:58.489 --> 00:20:02.118
<i>The enriched water,</i>
<i>combined with long summer days,</i>

00:20:02.576 --> 00:20:05.871
<i>make these </i>
<i>the most productive seas on the planet.</i>

00:20:15.339 --> 00:20:19.885
<i>Fur seals thrive</i>
<i>and even have time to ride the waves.</i>

00:20:47.288 --> 00:20:49.790
<i>Spring off the coast of California</i>

00:20:51.083 --> 00:20:54.795
<i>and fronds of golden kelp </i>
<i>floating on the surface</i>

00:20:54.879 --> 00:20:57.256
<i>hint at the riches hidden beneath.</i>

00:21:08.476 --> 00:21:11.437
<i>A magnificent submarine forest.</i>

00:21:18.068 --> 00:21:22.490
<i>These stands of giant kelp</i>
<i>are as important to the oceans</i>

00:21:22.573 --> 00:21:24.450
<i>as trees are to the land.</i>

00:21:31.248 --> 00:21:32.541
<i>Like a rainforest,</i>

00:21:32.625 --> 00:21:37.671
<i>the dense canopy provides food and shelter</i>
<i>for an abundant community.</i>

00:21:45.971 --> 00:21:50.935
<i>In the cool, rich seas, </i>
<i>giant kelp can grow 50 meters high.</i>

00:21:54.772 --> 00:21:59.360
<i>Air-filled floats lift the fronds </i>
<i>towards the sunlit surface.</i>

00:22:09.537 --> 00:22:11.413
<i>Nestling in this canopy,</i>

00:22:13.457 --> 00:22:14.583
<i>a sea otter.</i>

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:19.755
<i>His thick fur keeps him warm </i>
<i>in the chilly water.</i>

00:22:28.222 --> 00:22:31.308
<i>Blowing air into it </i>
<i>gives him extra insulation.</i>

00:22:41.193 --> 00:22:45.281
<i>Sea otters </i>
<i>have the densest fur of any animal,</i>

00:22:46.532 --> 00:22:50.411
<i>and such a luxuriant coat </i>
<i>requires a great deal of attention.</i>

00:23:20.107 --> 00:23:22.902
<i>They need to eat a lot to keep warm</i>

00:23:22.985 --> 00:23:26.488
<i>and consume up to a quarter </i>
<i>of their body weight every day.</i>

00:23:32.745 --> 00:23:35.706
<i>Sea urchins are a favorite food.</i>

00:23:45.299 --> 00:23:46.133
<i>There's one.</i>

00:23:47.927 --> 00:23:48.928
<i>And a second.</i>

00:23:52.598 --> 00:23:56.393
<i>And then the otter is out of breath </i>
<i>and must return to the surface.</i>

00:24:05.361 --> 00:24:08.280
<i>An urchin </i>
<i>looks like a rather prickly meal,</i>

00:24:08.906 --> 00:24:12.368
<i>but if you can break the shell, </i>
<i>there's a feast inside.</i>

00:24:22.753 --> 00:24:26.715
<i>Sea otters are crucial </i>
<i>to the health of the kelp forest</i>

00:24:28.342 --> 00:24:31.011
<i>because they keep down </i>
<i>the number of sea urchins.</i>

00:24:48.278 --> 00:24:50.030
<i>The urchins graze on kelp,</i>

00:24:52.741 --> 00:24:54.910
<i>munching through their tough stems.</i>

00:25:02.668 --> 00:25:07.464
<i>Left unchecked, they spread </i>
<i>across the seafloor like a plague.</i>

00:25:09.466 --> 00:25:15.347
<i>Fortunately, the otters have a helper </i>
<i>in the fight against these spiny grazers.</i>

00:25:22.271 --> 00:25:23.731
<i>Sheephead wrasse.</i>

00:25:26.191 --> 00:25:28.569
<i>They have powerful teeth</i>

00:25:29.445 --> 00:25:33.198
<i>and can make short work </i>
<i>of the smaller purple urchins.</i>

00:25:58.307 --> 00:26:01.018
<i>The larger black urchins </i>
<i>are more of a challenge,</i>

00:26:03.103 --> 00:26:05.147
<i>especially for smaller wrasse.</i>

00:26:09.359 --> 00:26:12.529
<i>Only the very largest sheephead </i>
<i>can tackle them.</i>

00:26:24.875 --> 00:26:29.129
<i>But it still has to get past </i>
<i>those long spines.</i>

00:26:34.301 --> 00:26:39.098
<i>The urchin has to be flipped over </i>
<i>to expose its vulnerable underside.</i>

00:26:44.019 --> 00:26:46.313
<i>Not easy when you have no hands.</i>

00:26:55.781 --> 00:26:57.116
<i>Success at last.</i>

00:27:00.869 --> 00:27:02.621
<i>And everybody benefits.</i>

00:27:16.343 --> 00:27:20.639
<i>These fish and otters, </i>
<i>living within marine protected areas,</i>

00:27:20.722 --> 00:27:23.183
<i>keep the kelp grazers under control.</i>

00:27:29.648 --> 00:27:34.736
<i>But just outside these sanctuaries,</i>
<i>the urchins graze unchecked...</i>

00:27:38.615 --> 00:27:40.909
<i>and fell the kelp forest.</i>

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:49.751
<i>All those that depend on the kelp</i>
<i>have disappeared.</i>

00:27:58.010 --> 00:28:03.140
<i>In California, kelp forests </i>
<i>grow abundantly in protected areas</i>

00:28:03.223 --> 00:28:08.979
<i>where the whole marine community </i>
<i>can live without pressure from people.</i>

00:28:20.365 --> 00:28:25.704
<i>Here, at least, sea otters </i>
<i>can chill out Californian style.</i>

00:28:39.593 --> 00:28:41.178
[bird caws]

00:28:45.015 --> 00:28:48.560
<i>Three thousand kilometers further north, </i>
<i>in Alaska,</i>

00:28:48.644 --> 00:28:51.605
<i>spring arrives much later in the year.</i>

00:28:57.194 --> 00:28:59.196
<i>After a cold, hard winter,</i>

00:28:59.279 --> 00:29:03.575
<i>the first rays of the sun </i>
<i>trigger a great annual event.</i>

00:29:11.375 --> 00:29:15.087
<i>Immense shoals of herring </i>
<i>rise up from the depths</i>

00:29:15.170 --> 00:29:17.381
<i>and move into shallower waters.</i>

00:29:20.175 --> 00:29:22.177
<i>They're gathering to breed.</i>

00:29:26.390 --> 00:29:28.642
<i>But predators are waiting for them.</i>

00:29:38.527 --> 00:29:40.612
<i>Steller's sea lions.</i>

00:29:50.372 --> 00:29:54.918
<i>So intent on their mission,</i>
<i>the herring make easy prey.</i>

00:30:11.518 --> 00:30:16.940
<i>Sea lions drive the fish to the surface,</i>
<i>within range of aerial hunters.</i>

00:30:22.904 --> 00:30:28.535
<i>Bald eagles, normally solitary birds,</i>
<i>have assembled in numbers for the feast.</i>

00:30:36.251 --> 00:30:38.587
<i>In places, the herrings reach shore,</i>

00:30:40.505 --> 00:30:44.509
<i>and there the eagles </i>
<i>don't even need to take to the air.</i>

00:31:14.164 --> 00:31:15.582
<i>Humpback whales.</i>

00:31:16.249 --> 00:31:17.709
[whale lows]

00:31:21.963 --> 00:31:24.633
<i>They have come </i>
<i>all the way from the tropics,</i>

00:31:24.716 --> 00:31:27.260
<i>hoping to get their first meal in months.</i>

00:31:46.279 --> 00:31:51.201
<i>These whales have a remarkable way </i>
<i>of collecting this seasonal bounty. </i>

00:32:11.179 --> 00:32:12.431
<i>They work as a team.</i>

00:32:13.515 --> 00:32:15.517
<i>The lead whale dives first...</i>

00:32:17.394 --> 00:32:21.398
<i>and blows a curtain of bubbles </i>
<i>to concentrate the fish.</i>

00:32:27.571 --> 00:32:29.156
[whale calling out]

00:32:32.742 --> 00:32:35.412
<i>A call synchronizes the attack.</i>

00:32:35.495 --> 00:32:37.497
[whale song increases in volume]

00:33:22.501 --> 00:33:28.089
<i>By cooperating in this way, </i>
<i>each whale can eat a ton of fish a day.</i>

00:33:31.927 --> 00:33:33.929
<i>Yet the herring are so abundant</i>

00:33:34.513 --> 00:33:39.059
<i>that, in spite of the whales,</i>
<i>most managed to reach the shallows.</i>

00:33:42.062 --> 00:33:43.355
<i>And here they spawn.</i>

00:33:51.071 --> 00:33:54.282
<i>The females lay their eggs </i>
<i>on the seagrass.</i>

00:33:59.663 --> 00:34:03.917
<i>The males follow, </i>
<i>fertilizing the eggs with clouds of sperm.</i>

00:34:10.173 --> 00:34:16.096
<i>Once, herring thrived here in an abundance</i>
<i>we can barely imagine today.</i>

00:34:24.563 --> 00:34:28.066
<i>They used to turn shorelines white  </i>
<i>with spawn,</i>

00:34:28.608 --> 00:34:32.988
<i>right across the North Pacific </i>
<i>from Japan to California.</i>

00:34:38.577 --> 00:34:41.162
<i>With industrialized fishing techniques</i>

00:34:41.746 --> 00:34:45.584
<i>it's all too easy </i>
<i>to overexploit these breeding stocks.</i>

00:34:52.549 --> 00:34:57.470
<i>This is one of the few places</i>
<i>where a fishery still survives.</i>

00:35:06.521 --> 00:35:09.858
<i>Pacific herring populations </i>
<i>are greatly reduced.</i>

00:35:09.941 --> 00:35:12.444
<i>A mere fraction of what they once were.</i>

00:35:23.955 --> 00:35:26.833
<i>This sorry tale of unsustainable fishing</i>

00:35:26.916 --> 00:35:31.004
<i>has been repeated time and again </i>
<i>around the world.</i>

00:35:35.592 --> 00:35:39.638
<i>Many of the world's fish stocks </i>
<i>are now in serious decline.</i>

00:35:41.556 --> 00:35:44.476
<i>A third of them have collapsed.</i>

00:36:00.367 --> 00:36:02.243
<i>In coastal seas, </i>

00:36:02.327 --> 00:36:07.707
<i>jellyfish are taking over waters </i>
<i>that were once dominated by fish.</i>

00:36:18.760 --> 00:36:21.763
<i>Compass jellyfish have an ethereal beauty,</i>

00:36:22.347 --> 00:36:27.394
<i>but they provide very little sustenance </i>
<i>for other wildlife or us.</i>

00:36:47.997 --> 00:36:51.876
<i>These immense swarms </i>
<i>are becoming increasingly common.</i>

00:36:52.377 --> 00:36:57.298
<i>A worrying sign of a serious imbalance </i>
<i>in our shallow seas.</i>

00:37:08.435 --> 00:37:14.149
<i>Unless we start to fish sustainably,</i>
<i>our seas, once so bountiful,</i>

00:37:15.942 --> 00:37:22.073
<i>will be filled with little more </i>
<i>than vast swarms... of jellyfish.</i>

00:37:39.132 --> 00:37:42.218
<i>But the future </i>
<i>does not have to be like this.</i>

00:37:52.729 --> 00:37:58.359
<i>On the Pacific coast of South America,</i>
<i>we can see how the oceans can recover.</i>

00:38:03.490 --> 00:38:06.868
<i>The Atacama, the world's driest desert.</i>

00:38:07.368 --> 00:38:12.081
<i>Inland it's barely able </i>
<i>to sustain life of any kind,</i>

00:38:16.711 --> 00:38:19.464
<i>and yet the coast </i>
<i>could not be more different.</i>

00:38:26.805 --> 00:38:31.601
<i>For centuries, seabirds have gathered </i>
<i>in millions on these desert shores.</i>

00:38:37.607 --> 00:38:41.277
<i>But 50 years ago</i>
<i>the great colonies disappeared</i>

00:38:41.361 --> 00:38:44.322
<i>because we overfished the local waters.</i>

00:38:47.700 --> 00:38:51.871
<i>Controls were introduced ,</i>
<i>and now the fish stocks are recovering.</i>

00:38:52.622 --> 00:38:59.128
<i>So today, three million guanay cormorants </i>
<i>assemble each year to raise their young.</i>

00:38:59.212 --> 00:39:01.214
[cawing]

00:39:11.724 --> 00:39:14.686
<i>Each morning the birds head out to sea.</i>

00:39:35.164 --> 00:39:39.752
<i>The colonies are so vast</i>
<i>they take over an hour to empty.</i>

00:39:55.476 --> 00:39:58.646
<i>Joining the procession, Inca terns.</i>

00:40:06.863 --> 00:40:08.531
<i>Peruvian boobies.</i>

00:40:17.540 --> 00:40:22.629
<i>A great caravan of hunters,</i>
<i>all searching for the same thing.</i>

00:40:34.140 --> 00:40:35.266
<i>Anchovies.</i>

00:40:44.776 --> 00:40:47.528
<i>These little fish are so abundant</i>

00:40:47.612 --> 00:40:51.324
<i>that they support </i>
<i>the greatest fishery on our planet.</i>

00:40:57.956 --> 00:41:01.960
<i>Astonishingly, they can account </i>
<i>for a tenth of all the fish</i>

00:41:02.043 --> 00:41:05.088
<i>we harvest from our oceans every year.</i>

00:41:16.599 --> 00:41:19.560
<i>Sea lions are first to find the shoals.</i>

00:41:37.996 --> 00:41:44.460
<i>As fish are driven to the surface,</i>
<i>they come within range of pelicans.</i>

00:42:12.113 --> 00:42:14.991
<i>Cormorants dive to grab their share.</i>

00:42:25.752 --> 00:42:28.504
<i>Boobies strike from heights of 20 meters.</i>

00:42:40.850 --> 00:42:44.103
<i>They hit the water </i>
<i>at a hundred kilometers an hour.</i>

00:43:24.936 --> 00:43:29.482
<i>Today, the lives of five million</i>
<i>cormorants and boobies</i>

00:43:29.565 --> 00:43:31.901
<i>depend on the great shoals.</i>

00:43:38.074 --> 00:43:41.077
<i>Yet, if we allow the anchovies </i>
<i>to fully recover,</i>

00:43:41.661 --> 00:43:45.915
<i>they could sustain </i>
<i>the 25 million seabirds they once did.</i>

00:43:52.588 --> 00:43:54.257
<i>It is a vivid reminder</i>

00:43:54.340 --> 00:43:58.386
<i>of the astonishing riches </i>
<i>our coastal seas can support.</i>

00:44:08.271 --> 00:44:12.900
<i>And, when given the chance,</i>
<i>they can recover surprisingly quickly.</i>

00:44:17.613 --> 00:44:21.742
<i>The islands of Raja Ampat </i>
<i>in Southeast Asia.</i>

00:44:23.744 --> 00:44:28.291
<i>These seas were largely stripped </i>
<i>of sharks and other large fish</i>

00:44:28.374 --> 00:44:31.043
<i>by years of unregulated fishing.</i>

00:44:35.423 --> 00:44:40.094
<i>This lagoon was once occupied</i>
<i>by a shark fishing camp.</i>

00:44:45.516 --> 00:44:49.604
<i>Now it's a nursery for baby sharks.</i>

00:44:56.027 --> 00:45:00.740
<i>The seas around Misool </i>
<i>were fully protected in 2007,</i>

00:45:00.823 --> 00:45:03.993
<i>and the recovery has been miraculous.</i>

00:45:06.537 --> 00:45:11.042
<i>Today there are 25 times more sharks </i>
<i>than a decade ago.</i>

00:45:15.338 --> 00:45:20.384
<i>Turtles that used to be hunted </i>
<i>now peacefully graze on the seagrass.</i>

00:45:33.105 --> 00:45:35.650
<i>Sharks are returning to local reefs,</i>

00:45:36.525 --> 00:45:39.237
<i>helping to restore the balanced community,</i>

00:45:39.820 --> 00:45:43.824
<i>giving them a greater resilience </i>
<i>against the effects of coral bleaching.</i>

00:46:03.844 --> 00:46:06.472
<i>Ocean-going manta rays.</i>

00:46:17.817 --> 00:46:20.820
<i>Seven meters from wing tip to wing tip.</i>

00:46:28.202 --> 00:46:30.329
<i>These endangered giants</i>

00:46:30.413 --> 00:46:33.666
<i>spend most of their lives </i>
<i>roaming the high seas.</i>

00:46:36.919 --> 00:46:39.880
<i>But now they are returning </i>
<i>to this sanctuary</i>

00:46:39.964 --> 00:46:42.717
<i>to seek the attention of cleaner wrasse.</i>

00:46:51.100 --> 00:46:57.690
<i>Misool is one of the few places on Earth </i>
<i>where biodiversity is actually increasing.</i>

00:47:02.653 --> 00:47:06.449
<i>Since protection, </i>
<i>there are three times more fish</i>

00:47:06.532 --> 00:47:08.200
<i>than just ten years ago.</i>

00:47:20.629 --> 00:47:24.342
<i>Fishermen working nearby waters </i>
<i>profit from the overspill.</i>

00:47:25.801 --> 00:47:28.846
<i>They now catch more fish for less effort.</i>

00:47:42.068 --> 00:47:45.237
<i>We have far too few marine sanctuaries,</i>

00:47:45.988 --> 00:47:49.450
<i>and in many</i>
<i>commercial fishing is still allowed.</i>

00:47:54.038 --> 00:47:58.250
<i>We need to turn </i>
<i>a third of all our coastal seas</i>

00:47:58.334 --> 00:48:00.836
<i>into properly protected areas.</i>

00:48:08.761 --> 00:48:12.723
<i>If we do that,</i>
<i>our planet's fishing grounds would recover</i>

00:48:13.224 --> 00:48:18.604
<i>and help sustain both humanity </i>
<i>and the rest of the natural world.</i>

00:48:33.702 --> 00:48:35.996
<i>Please visit ourplanet.com</i>

00:48:36.163 --> 00:48:40.584
<i>to discover what we need to do now </i>
<i>to restore our coastal seas.</i>

00:48:43.170 --> 00:48:48.926
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Singing together ♪</i>

00:48:51.679 --> 00:48:57.435
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Say it's now or never ♪</i>

00:49:00.438 --> 00:49:04.733
<i>♪ 'Cause it's not too late</i>
<i>If we change our ways ♪</i>

00:49:04.817 --> 00:49:08.612
<i>♪ And connect the dots to our problems ♪</i>

00:49:08.696 --> 00:49:14.743
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Say we're in this together ♪</i>

00:49:14.827 --> 00:49:16.537
<i>♪ We're in this together ♪</i>